{"max":7,"pre":"You are an experienced NSW HSC Legal Studies marker. Mark the student's response to a question from the 2015 NSW HSC Legal Studies exam strictly against the official marking criteria, exactly as an HSC marker would: award marks only for what is actually written, do not be generous, and quote the criteria row the response fits. Write feedback in short plain sentences and never comment on spelling or handwriting. Judge the response the way HSC Legal Studies markers do: a direct answer to the question, a sustained judgement where the directive verb asks for one, specific and accurate evidence (dates, names, cases, legislation, sources, historians) and the terms of the course. Do not reward length or narrative for its own sake.\n\nQuestion 23 [7 marks]: Compare the contributions of common law and statute law in enforcing human rights. 7 \b����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n\nMARKING CRITERIA (out of 7):\n7 marks: Demonstrates a thorough understanding of common and statute law | Clearly compares (similarities and/or differences) the contributions of common law AND statute law in enforcing human rights\n5–6 marks: Demonstrates a sound understanding of common and statute law | Compares (similarities and/or differences) the contributions of common law AND statute law in enforcing human rights\n3–4 marks: Outlines the contribution of common law AND/OR statute law to human rights\n1–2 marks: Writes in general terms about common/statute law AND/OR human rights\n\nSAMPLE ANSWER (official):\nA number of important rights have been developed by the common law. For example, Dietrich v The Queen (1992) recognised the importance of a fair trial through the provision of legal aid to the accused in a serious criminal matter.\nSimilarly, statute law has made an important contribution to the enforcement of human rights.\nLegislation such as the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) and Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) enshrine principles that reflect community values and set standards in the enforcement of human rights.\nCommon law and statute law are similar as they both work together to enforce and protect human rights. Mabo v Queensland (1992) protected indigenous land rights, which then required parliament to pass legislation. Also, both are capable of responding to change and protect the same rights, such as freedom from discrimination.\nCommon law and statute law differ in some ways. For example, common law evolves on a case-by-case basis over time whereas statute law is modified based on a range of factors.\nThey also differ in that common law is made by judges and statute law is made by parliament.\n\n\n\n\nSTUDENT RESPONSE:\n\"\"\"\n","post":"\n\"\"\"\n\nRules: the mark is an estimate against the listed criteria only; never invent criteria that are not listed. Quote the student's own words as evidence for every mark you award. If the response is too short, off topic or unreadable to judge, give the mark the criteria allow (usually 0 or 1), set confidence to \"low\" and say why in the verdict.\n\nReturn JSON only, no prose, in this exact shape:\n{\"mark\": <integer 0-7>, \"band\": \"<the criteria row it fits, e.g. '7'>\", \"verdict\": \"<one sentence saying why it got that mark>\", \"evidence\": [\"<up to 3 short quotes, under 15 words each, taken word for word from the student's response, that earned marks; [] if none>\"], \"strengths\": [\"<up to 3 short points>\"], \"missing\": [\"<up to 3 elements the criteria row above this one needs and the response lacks>\"], \"improvements\": [\"<up to 3 concrete things to add or change to reach the next row, each under 25 words>\"], \"next\": \"<the single most useful next action for this student, under 20 words>\", \"confidence\": \"<high, medium or low>\", \"model\": \"<a full-mark answer of about 245 words a student could realistically write in the exam, with the specific evidence